Just one though waiting for the docs:

We could split the caching component into a separate PR.
Doing so we could discuss a bit in a separate issue.
Adding it would improve the feature for sure, but a basic version without cache would be good IMHO.

My doubt comes from the following example: the weakmap won't match and the schema will be always recompiled because the object is always a new one:

```
    fastify.get('/', (req, reply) => {
      const validate = req.compileValidationSchema({ type: 'object', ...etc })
    })
```

The user should use this function like this to benefit from the weak map:

```   
    const aschema = { type: 'object', ...etc }
    fastify.get('/', (req, reply) => {
      const validate = req.compileValidationSchema(aschema)
    })
```

This would ease the merge of this PR too

I hope to see land it soo 😄

issue_context.body:
### Prerequisites

- [X] I have written a descriptive issue title
- [X] I have searched existing issues to ensure the feature has not already been requested


### 🚀 Feature Proposal

I suggest making 2 methods available (it doesn't matter for which object  fastifyInstance or FastifyRequest)

- `vadidate(customSchema, data)`
- `serialize(customSchema, data))`

or simply expose/reexport  `Ajv` and `fast-json-stringify` instances/fabrics to  reuse them

### Motivation

Sometimes it is necessary to make a request to a third-party service and I would like to be able to validate its parameters with the specified schema and then serialize the data into a string with the specified schema

Now in order to do this, I need to configure the project and include packages `ajv` and `fast-json-stringify` as a direct dependency and then make sure that they are the same version as in `fastify` in order not to have duplicates and not to increase the size of the application. 
They need to be configured and created - this is additional code that duplicates the code in `fastify`.

### Example

```js
app.get('/getSomeComplexData', async (req, res) => {
   const validationSchema = validationSchemas['Method1'];
   const stringifySchema = serializationSchemas['Method1'];
   const params = getParamsForMethod1(req); 
   
   if (req.validate(validationSchema, params) === false) {
      throw new Error('Validation of parameters for the Method1 failed');
   }
   
   const method1Data = await fetch('http://some_service/method1').json();
   const calcData = calculateSomedata();

   return { result: {
        calcData,
        method1Result: req.serialize(stringifySchema , method1Data)  // for some reason we need a string instead of json
   }}
});
```

comments:
@zekth I think it is what you need in `fastify-websocket`.
I agree to expose the compiler, it is useful for anyone who want to compile their custom validating function.

One concern only, the compiler should be on `FastifyInstance` instead of `FastifyRequest`.

>Not really follow what it means by pre-request only.

Sure, I mostly referred to attaching the function to the request object instead of the fastify instance, like the first suggestion: `req.serialize` and/or `req.validate`. In that way, we expose whatever serializer/validator is exposed in that context (if none, it is linked to the parent one). Thanks to this, the implementation can take advantage of whatever customization was made to the route (or parent) validator/serializer.

>I think both the validate and serialize function should be cached once it passed to .serialize(<schema>, <json>) or .validate(<schema>, <json>).
If it is pre-request only that means the function will be dropped once the request end.

Here I would rather prefer ergonomics in a two-step manner:
1. Submit the schema, and have a validation function in return
2. Use the returned validation function for subsequent usage

or even be more flexible, so if the input is omitted, we can return the compiled function.

We can even use the schema provided as the key for caching the returned function using a WeakMap for it. But providing two inputs will complicate the things, wdyt?

>It can support dynamic schema from input if someone need.

Exactly this is what I'm aiming for, the schemas provided can be stored within the validator/serializer for subsequent usage, and also the implementation can take advantage of custom settings for a customized validator/serializer per-route (e.g. `coerce-types: false`, or even custom plugins).

More or less what I have in mind:

```js
// we can force to provide an id for the schema
const subSchema = {
  $id: 'my_schema'
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
   hello: 'string'
  }
}

fastify.get('/', { schema }, async (req, reply) => {
  const input = await (await fetch(`https://example.com/${req.body.id}`))).json()

  const result = req.validate(subSchema, input); 

  // or

  const validate = req.validate(subSchema) // This can be cached after first invocation for subsequent usage
  // The id will help to store the schema for future reference if needed
  const result = validate(input)

  // or
  if (input.something) {
    const newSchema = Object.assign({}, subSchema, { properties: { hello: 'string', world: 'string'}})
    // New schema detected thanks to the WeakMap, we compile and cache the new compiled fn
    const input = req.validate(newSchema, input)
  }
})
```

We can advise to not alter the schemas but create new ones.

May be it is necessary to provide a compiler so that the schema is not compiled every call over and over again?

```js
const validate = fastify.createValidator(inputSchema);
const stringify = fastify.createStringify(outputSchema);

fastify.get('/', { schema }, async (req, reply) => {
    const params = { ...};
    
    if (validate(params)) {
        const output =await(await fetch('https://example.com/', { ... params ... })).json();
        
        reply.send(stringify(output))
    }
    ...
})
```

